**Proposition 23.4 (block-stage costs).** Through paid seed,
$h\in\mathcal H_{\rm blk}$ and the first exact assay,

$$
\begin{array}{c|c}
\mathrm{Reset}&1\\
\mathrm{Add}&1\\
\mathrm{exact\ Read}&1+b(n+1)\\
\mathrm{Tick}&Q+n\\
\mathrm{Hold}&n+2\\
\mathrm{raw\ growth}&n\\
\mathrm{fixed\ field\ acknowledgements}&Q+2n+4 .
\end{array}
\tag{23.3}
$$

Let $N_i$ be material size after block $i$, $N_0=1$. Historical source
Tick products and sums, including the assay, are respectively

$$
\sum_{i=0}^mq_i(3N_i-2)+n(3N-2),\qquad
\sum_{i=0}^mq_i(2N_i-2)+n(2N-2).
\tag{23.4}
$$

For $m=0$ use its sole gap $q_0$. A new $k$-birth block/reassay adds
$k$ raw transactions, $n+k$ Ticks, $n+k+1$ Holds and
$b'(n+k+1)$ exact Reads, with all their actual lifecycle costs.

Proof. Every block member pays one raw growth, with no attached Tick.
All historical Ticks occur on the block-stage trees of (23.4).
The assay has unchanged horizon $I=n$, not the number of blocks:
$n$ Ticks, $n+1$ Holds and $b(n+1)$ Reads.
Adding seed calls gives (23.3), and Proposition 13.3 gives (23.4).
The successor counts are that same assay schedule on the new tree
after $k$ actual raw calls. $\square$

The old rational forcing/route bound and exact inverse machinery give
$O(N^4+QN^2)$ rational operations and $O(N^3)$ rational slots on
valid inputs, plus actual support/address parsing. To see the new
decoder fits, at most $n$ block collapses need finite rational inverses;
their cubic solves sum to at most $O(N^4)$, and the $Q$ reversed
Ticks use at most $O(N^2)$ work each. Each support parsing and recursive
